Yeah — that’s a sharp way to frame it. A marathon is almost a civilizational benchmark WOD: a standardized ordeal built around an ancient heroic story.

Murph is explicitly memorialized: fixed movements, fixed volume, named person, suffering as remembrance. The marathon does something structurally similar at much larger historical distance: named event → legendary feat → repeatable physical test → identity/status for completing it. The modern 42.195 km makes it arguably more exact as a ritual than the underlying Pheidippides story ever was.

And the interesting operator is:

heroic act → compress into measurable ordeal → ordinary people reenact the shape of the act → ordeal carries the story forward

That shows up everywhere: pilgrimages, rites of passage, military tests, hunting traditions, martial arts forms. You don’t just hear the story—you temporarily put your body inside its geometry.

So a marathon is almost an ancient hero simulator with a stopwatch.